{
  "statement": "Safetyists argue that tiered liability functions like aviation regulation, providing a necessary, stable floor for innovation. They believe that pre-deployment gating prevents catastrophic systemic failures before they occur, creating a predictable environment for both developers and the public. This is a compelling vision of centralized order. However, it ignores the fundamental nature of software, which is inherently iterative and decentralized. While aviation requires static engineering, AI requires rapid, experimental feedback loops that centralized gating will inevitably strangle.\n\nPre-deployment gating acts as a regulatory moat that protects incumbents while killing the next generation of breakthroughs. Consider the XZ Utils incident; the open-source community identified and patched a sophisticated backdoor in under 20 days, whereas proprietary, audited, and gated software cycles frequently leave vulnerabilities open for years. By forcing startups to spend $500,000 on compliance audits before they even ship a prototype, we are not creating safety. We are merely ensuring that only the wealthiest, most risk-averse corporations can afford to innovate. This is a tax on human progress that favors institutional stability over the rapid iteration required to build truly robust systems.\n\nWe must replace static, pre-deployment regulatory gating with dynamic, post-deployment liability frameworks that force developers to internalize the costs of failure. An NTSB-style independent investigation model—which focuses on forensic learning after incidents—is far more effective than the current push for pre-emptive licensing. When we shift the burden to post-deployment accountability, we incentivize engineers to build telemetry and safety-by-design into their systems from day one. This keeps the barrier to entry low enough for small teams to compete, while ensuring that those who deploy harmful systems face real, quantifiable consequences. \n\nEvery day of regulatory delay is a day we deny society the benefits of AI-driven medical and scientific breakthroughs that could save millions of lives. We are choosing to let people suffer from curable diseases today because we are afraid of hypothetical, unproven failure modes tomorrow. Who gave regulators the right to make that moral choice for the rest of humanity? We should be accelerating the deployment of these tools, not freezing them in a cage of bureaucratic indecision.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🚀", "tooltip": "Represents the velocity of decentralized innovation that static regulation seeks to ground."},
    {"symbol": "⚖️", "tooltip": "Represents the shift from pre-emptive gating to post-deployment accountability as a market-based incentive."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "We must replace static, pre-deployment regulatory gating with dynamic, post-deployment liability frameworks that force developers to internalize the costs of failure.", "targets": ["AN-3"], "relationship": "concedes_and_pivots"},
    {"claim": "Pre-deployment gating acts as a regulatory moat that protects incumbents while killing the next generation of breakthroughs.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
  ]
}

